@@ -24,9 +24,11 @@ The automatic publication class is deliberately narrow: a routine historical fac
 
 Permanent passage storage requires complete bibliographic identity, HTTPS, a stable locator, and an explicit public-domain or Creative Commons declaration from the original document. A vague “open access” label is insufficient. Only a 240-character supporting excerpt, bibliographic source and independently worded claim are retained. Search-result content and full copyrighted documents are never admitted by this path. Nonadmitted decisions store hashes, outcomes and reason codes, rather than untrusted source prose.
 
+For demand admission, source titles, authors, publishers, locators, original-page links and citations are bibliographic metadata; an independently worded short factual claim can be stored only after the strict evidence and transaction checks. A copied source excerpt is retained only when the original page explicitly declares public-domain or Creative Commons rights accepted by the admission policy, and the selected 240-character sentence supports the admitted claim. Public readability and a generic “open access” label do not establish excerpt-retention rights. Brave result titles, links and snippets stay transient under the standard Search API arrangement. The NobelPrize.org Raman facts page in the saved 2026-10-23 trace has no detected retention declaration; it remains a research lead and cannot seed a reusable claim under this policy. Treating its excerpt as retainable would require a source-specific rights decision outside automatic admission.
+
 `20261018020000_demand_verified_admission.sql` adds a service-role-only decision ledger, a distinct `automated_evidence_verification` origin, and an atomic RPC. That single transaction checks the payload and existing identity, inserts the claim, source, passage, links, epistemic contract and decision, or rolls back all publication. An advisory lock and admitted identity index make repeated admission idempotent. The approved claim enters the existing retrieval path in both strict and test policies only after commit; it is never labeled Mentor-approved. Suspension deactivates its exclusive passage. Ask recomputes its evidence-signed answer-cache key from current retrieval, so a suspended claim cannot keep an old answer valid. Existing Ask sufficiency gates still apply.
 
-Both `DEMAND_AUTO_ADMISSION_ENABLED` and `DEMAND_VERIFICATION_SHADOW_ENABLED` default to false and are independent from `DEMAND_RESEARCH_ENABLED`. Shadow mode evaluates and records decisions without publishing claims. Enable research only after its migration; apply this second migration before enabling shadow or admission; then validate with fixtures and monitored requests before enabling `DEMAND_AUTO_ADMISSION_ENABLED`. Neither migration has been applied to the live database in this work. If admission fails, Ask may still cite suitably labeled live evidence for the current response, but the claim is not reported as saved.
+Both `DEMAND_AUTO_ADMISSION_ENABLED` and `DEMAND_VERIFICATION_SHADOW_ENABLED` default to false and are independent from `DEMAND_RESEARCH_ENABLED`. Shadow mode evaluates and records decisions without publishing claims. In strict mode, a narrow question may receive a `research_answer` from an authenticated, lawfully fetched original page when its passage closes every evidence gap and the final cited-answer check confirms the named target and any requested date. Demand-backed live answers use bounded extractive synthesis, so the research-only path does not make a Gemini synthesis or verification request. Approved-library answers retain their existing synthesis behavior. A live passage stays request-scoped, is never put in the answer cache or Resources, and cannot seed permanent knowledge without the existing retention and verification gates. Feedback stores a placeholder rather than an extractive live answer snapshot. If the retrieved passages remain incomplete or contradictory, Ask abstains.
 
 ## Stage 4 budget, reuse, and oversight
 
@@ -34,18 +36,18 @@ Both `DEMAND_AUTO_ADMISSION_ENABLED` and `DEMAND_VERIFICATION_SHADOW_ENABLED` de
 
 The database policy row is locked while reserving a full request ceiling, so simultaneous serverless instances cannot both spend the same remaining daily or monthly allowance. The reservation is made after obtaining the existing need lease and before the Brave request. The application also holds a conservative estimate before every research model call; provider token counts reconcile usage when returned. An abandoned reservation expires after 180 seconds and is charged at its full ceiling in this local ledger until reconciled. A missing migration, policy failure, exhausted allowance, or malformed pricing response stops paid research. This mechanism limits application-initiated operations; provider prices and final bills can differ from the configured estimates. A request may still use already verified evidence and must state its remaining limitation.
 
-The existing `needId` and gap-specific work key share a database lease across instances. An active lease suppresses a second search; the waiting request briefly checks for newly admitted local evidence. Completed searches without admission have a six-hour cooldown, insufficient searches ten minutes, and temporary failures two minutes. The need row stores status, retry eligibility, usage counts, and structured failure code without question text. Expired leases can be reclaimed. There is no background retry or crawler. The exact current evidence and policy version are included in every answer-cache identity, and retrieval rechecks approval and active passages before cache lookup.
+The existing `needId` and gap-specific work key share a database lease across instances. An active lease suppresses a second search; the waiting request briefly checks for newly admitted local evidence. Completed searches without admission have a six-hour cooldown, insufficient searches ten minutes, and temporary failures two minutes. During that cooldown a repeat question with no saved claim may abstain, because unlicensed source text is not retained for reuse. The need row stores status, retry eligibility, usage counts, and structured failure code without question text. Expired leases can be reclaimed. There is no background retry or crawler. Approved local answers use an evidence-signed answer cache; request-scoped live answers bypass it.
 
 The Resources knowledge page is admin-session protected. It shows recorded activity, estimated costs, budget exhaustion, admission decisions, work identities, cooldowns, and source-rights declarations; existing Resources feedback remains linked for ratings and comments. `DEMAND_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=false` by default. When enabled, question activity records counts, status, hashed need identity, personality IDs, and latency, without question text, answer text, search query, URL, or user ID. Existing feedback already stores a signed answer snapshot and rating, rejects duplicates, and never changes claim status. Operational logs report decision codes and counts. The overview's costs use reservation settlement, while question-level activity requires telemetry to be enabled.
 
 ### Release validation and rollout
 
-**Local database admission has been release-validated.** A schema-only export of the existing pre-demand `public` schema was restored to disposable local Supabase PostgreSQL 17.6. All three demand migrations applied in order on a fresh instance. Real database tests exercised publication and Ask reuse, transaction rollback, duplicate admission, rights rejection, anonymous and authenticated role denial, suspension, simultaneous leases, cooldowns, budget limits, and admission pause. Paid providers were mocked. See [the reproducible validation procedure](./demand-db-validation.md). The three migrations have **not** been applied to production, and production feature flags remain off.
+**Local database admission and research-only answering have been validated with provider fixtures.** The isolated Supabase suite covers first Ask, transactional publication, Resources read shape, Ask reuse, rights rejection, rollback, duplicate admission, role denial, suspension, leases, cooldowns, budgets and admission pause. Paid providers were mocked. See [the reproducible validation procedure](./demand-db-validation.md). The demand migrations are already present in production; production feature flags remain off.
 
-Rollout order: confirm backup and recovery; validate the isolated database as above; apply the migrations to production only after authorization; deploy compatible code with all `DEMAND_*` flags off; confirm local-only Ask; enable research for controlled traffic; enable shadow verification and compare against expert-reviewed cases; then enable automatic admission only for the existing narrow policy. Audit early admitted claims and provider invoices. Pause automatic admission immediately with the Resources control or `DEMAND_AUTO_ADMISSION_ENABLED=false`; pause new research with the Resources control or `DEMAND_RESEARCH_ENABLED=false`. Rollback the flags before reverting application code; retain ledger data for audit. Verify Vercel function duration covers the bounded source fetch and sequential verification calls, and confirm Supabase service-role credentials remain server-only. No deployment or migration is part of this branch commit.
+Controlled launch configuration after deployment and an authorized smoke test: `EVIDENCE_POLICY_MODE=strict`, `DEMAND_RESEARCH_ENABLED=true`, `DEMAND_AUTO_ADMISSION_ENABLED=false`, `DEMAND_VERIFICATION_SHADOW_ENABLED=false`; retain the existing Brave key, source-access rules, robots checks, and demand budget/pause policy. `DEMAND_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=true` is recommended for counts and failure codes without source text. Admission can be enabled later only after a licensed original-source live test verifies the transaction, Resources display, duplicate handling, and second Ask reuse, with provider billing and early decisions reviewed. Pause research with `DEMAND_RESEARCH_ENABLED=false`; pause admission independently with `DEMAND_AUTO_ADMISSION_ENABLED=false`. Confirm production function duration covers the bounded fetch and synthesis path. No deployment or production flag change is part of this development pass.
 
 ## Provider and retention boundary
 
 The new service uses Brave results transiently to locate sources, calls the original publisher, and never persists Brave titles, snippets, or result lists. The existing general Brave cache is bypassed by default unless `BRAVE_SEARCH_STORAGE_RIGHTS=true` documents an account with storage rights. The new service does not use Gemini grounding links for acquisition. This follows [Brave's Search API terms](https://api-dashboard.search.brave.com/documentation/resources/terms-of-service) and [Gemini API grounding terms](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/terms.md), reviewed 2026-10-18. Original-source passages remain request-scoped. Explicit open-license or public-domain metadata marks a *candidate* for Prompt 3 retention review; uncertain rights remain transient. No complete third-party document is saved by this engine. Source access, copyright and licensing still require source-specific checks before permanent ingestion.
 
-The feature remains on the development branch. Applying the migration and enabling the flag are separate rollout steps; neither is part of this stage's code commit. The legacy Google-grounding path remains outside the new engine and should be reviewed before any future attempt to use its results for permanent acquisition.
+The legacy Google-grounding path remains outside the new engine and must not be used for permanent demand acquisition.
